new file mode 100644
index 0000000..6596762
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/UPGRADE.md
@@ -0,0 +1,42 @@
+# Upgrade record
+
+## Rollout invariants
+
+- Preserve the exact production `secret_key_base` across every deployment and rollback. Do not regenerate it during a framework transition.
+- Deploy one framework release at a time. Rails 8.1 is the current release; the deployed Rails 7.2 release remains the rollback target.
+- Keep the shared cache and production database across deploys. A cache miss is acceptable, but an entry that raises while being decoded is not.
+- Keep every compatibility rotation until its old state can no longer be presented. Mail-link rotations must remain for at least 30 days after the final mail was sent in that format. Browser session age cannot be inferred from the mail lifetime.
+
+## Release history
+
+The preceding production release moved from Ruby 3.0/Rails 6.1 to Ruby 3.4/Rails 7.2 without changing the session secret. It established SHA-256/AES-GCM cookies as the current writer and retained readers for Rails 6.1 SHA-1/AES-CBC cookies and Rails 6.1 mail-link keys. Rails 7.2 deliberately wrote its cache, signed messages, record marshalling, and cookies in formats that Rails 7.0 could read while the Rails 8.1 bundle was evaluated through `Gemfile.next`.
+
+## Current release: Ruby 3.4 / Rails 8.1
+
+The primary bundle now locks Rails 8.1.4.1 and Ruby 3.4.8. `Gemfile.next`, `Gemfile.next.lock`, the alternate-bundle conditional in `Gemfile`, and the CI matrix for the alternate boot have been removed. The Roadie 3.4, Nokogiri 1.19, and SQLite 2.7 constraints retained from the candidate release are required by Rails 8.1 and remain in the single bundle.
+
+`config/application.rb` loads Rails 8.1 framework defaults with no serialized-format override. In particular, this release uses:
+
+- the Rails 7.1 cache format;
+- `:json_allow_marshal` for application messages with the JSON metadata envelope;
+- the Rails 7.1 Active Record marshalling format; and
+- JSON cookie serialization with authenticated AES-GCM encryption and purpose metadata.
+
+Rails 8.1's behavioral defaults are also active: strict freshness, path-relative redirect protection, JSON/JavaScript escaping, missing finder-order checks, the Ruby view tracker, and removal of hidden-field autocomplete. The application compatibility work exercises both the Rails 8.1 class-attribute and form-rendering behavior rather than overriding those defaults.
+
+All application use of the old positional Active Record `serialize` API now uses explicit `coder` and `type` keywords. Application deprecations use a registered Redmine deprecator instead of replacing the removed `ActiveSupport::Deprecation.warn` class API. The Rails-version branches used only to run the 8.1 candidate alongside Rails 7.2 have also been removed from migration, view, job-test, and serialization code.
+
+## Rollback and state compatibility
+
+No framework-default override is needed to roll this release back to the deployed Rails 7.2 configuration. The Rails 7.2 rollback uses its existing Marshal/hybrid-cookie readers and accepts the Rails 8.1 cache, mail-link, cookie, and Active Record formats. Conversely, Rails 8.1 accepts the old Rails 7.2 outputs: cache format 7.0, Marshal mail links with the legacy metadata envelope, Active Record marshalling format 6.1, and JSON cookies emitted by the former hybrid serializer. Bidirectional compatibility probes cover those formats in `test/unit/lib/redmine/rails_compatibility_test.rb`.
+
+The Rails 6.1 compatibility reader is still required for state older than the Rails 7.2 release. `Redmine::RailsCompatibility` keeps the Rails 6.1 SHA-1 key derivations, AES-CBC encrypted-cookie rotation, signed-cookie rotation, and mail-link verifier rotation. A narrowly scoped cookie reader also accepts a valid legacy Marshal cookie and reserializes it as JSON; the configured and newly written Rails 8.1 cookie serializer remains `:json`. This is a state reader, not a framework-default override.
+
+The same production `secret_key_base` must be present for every 8.1 deployment and 7.2 rollback. The existing-secret rollout must retain an existing session cookie, a 30-day unwatch link, and shared cache entries from both sides of the transition.
+
+## Next release
+
+1. Keep the Rails 6.1 cookie and mail-link rotations until production evidence shows that no corresponding state can still be presented. In particular, do not remove the SHA-1 mail rotation until at least 30 days after the last Rails 6.1 mail link was sent.
+2. Retain the Marshal cookie reader while any Rails 6.1 session cookie can remain live. Once that window has closed, remove the reader and the cookie/key rotations in `lib/redmine/rails_compatibility.rb`; 8.1 and 7.2 current-format cookies already use the same SHA-256/AES-GCM keys and JSON payloads.
+3. Record the production deploy/rollback probe results before removing any compatibility reader: use the same secret, an existing session, a pre-upgrade mail link, and cache entries written by both Rails 7.2 and Rails 8.1.
+4. Prepare the next Ruby or Rails release only as a single step after this Rails 8.1 release is the established rollback target. Ruby 4-era `benchmark`/`ostruct` standard-gem warnings from dependencies are not Rails framework deprecations and remain work for the Ruby-4 boundary.
